  "prediction": "**Aegis Ballistic Missile Defense (BMD) Program: Background and Issues for Congress**\n\nThe Aegis BMD program is a critical component of the United States' ballistic missile defense (BMD) capabilities, carried out by the Missile Defense Agency (MDA) and the Navy. The program equips Navy Aegis cruisers and destroyers with the capability to conduct BMD operations, using the Aegis ship combat system and BMD interceptor missiles. The issue for Congress is whether to approve, reject, or modify Department of Defense (DOD) acquisition strategies and proposed funding levels for the Aegis BMD program.\n\n**Program Overview**\n\nThe Aegis system was originally developed in the 1970s for defending ships against aircraft, anti-ship cruise missiles, surface threats, and subsurface threats. The system has undergone numerous updates and modernizations, with the latest variants incorporating advanced radar and missile systems. The Aegis BMD system exists in several variants, with increasing capabilities, including the 3.6.X, 4.0.3, 4.1, 5.0 CU, 5.1, and 6.X variants.\n\n**BMD Capabilities**\n\nThe Aegis BMD system is designed to intercept theater-range ballistic missiles, with the potential to also intercept intercontinental ballistic missiles (ICBMs). The system uses the Standard Missile-3 (SM-3) interceptor, which is equipped with a \"hit-to-kill\" warhead. The SM-3 Block IIA version has a larger diameter and increased range, making it more capable of intercepting ICBMs.\n\n**International Cooperation**\n\nThe United States has cooperated with Japan on the development of the SM-3 Block IIA missile, with Japan funding a significant portion of the development costs. Japan plans to modify all six of its Aegis destroyers to include the Aegis BMD capability and has also announced plans to procure two Aegis Ashore systems.\n\n**Future Developments**\n\nThe Navy is exploring the use of ship-based lasers, electromagnetic railguns (EMRGs), and gun-launched guided projectiles (GLGPs) to contribute to terminal-phase BMD operations. The MDA is also evaluating the potential for the Aegis Ashore Missile Defense Test Center in Hawaii to be operationalized as a land-based Aegis BMD site to strengthen the defense of Hawaii against North Korean missile threats.\n\n**Technical Risks and Test and Evaluation Issues**\n\nThe Aegis BMD program has faced technical risks and test and evaluation issues",
